    rationale: "Retroperitoneal bleeding is a life-threatening complication of femoral artery cannulation occurring when the arterial puncture site is above the inguinal ligament. Blood tracks silently into the retroperitoneal space; external bleeding may be completely absent. Hallmarks are unexplained hypotension, tachycardia, and severe ipsilateral flank or lower back pain. Emergency management includes rapid IV fluids, manual pressure proximal to the puncture, and CT confirmation. Why the other options fail: Urinary retention gives suprapubic discomfort and a distended bladder, not flank and back pain with hypotension; a musculoskeletal strain does not produce hypotension; coronary re-occlusion gives chest pain, not back pain with a falling pressure. The picture is retroperitoneal bleeding.",
