    correctChoiceId: "c",
    rationale: "Daily morning weights are the single most sensitive, objective, and earliest indicator of fluid retention in heart failure. 1 liter of retained fluid equals 1 kilogram (2.2 lbs) of body weight. The golden rule for heart failure clients is: weigh every morning on the same scale, wearing similar clothing, after voiding and before breakfast; report a weight gain of 2-3 lbs in 24 hours or 5 lbs in 1 week. Why the other options fail: Weighing weekly on different scales is too infrequent and too inconsistent to detect retention; abdominal girth every evening is not the validated daily measure and fluctuates with meals; a pulse change of five beats is not the earliest marker. Daily morning weights on one scale are the standard.",
