    rationale: "Acute cardiac tamponade occurs when blood or pericardial effusion accumulates in the rigid pericardial sac, compressing the cardiac chambers and preventing ventricular filling during diastole. It is recognized clinically by Beck's Triad: 1) Hypotension with narrowed pulse pressure, 2) Jugular venous distention (JVD), and 3) Muffled/distant heart sounds, plus pulsus paradoxus (> 10 mmHg inspiratory drop in SBP). Emergency treatment is pericardiocentesis. Why the other options fail: A pneumothorax gives absent breath sounds on the affected side rather than muffled heart sounds; aortic dissection gives tearing pain with a pulse deficit; hypertrophic cardiomyopathy gives a systolic murmur and syncope. Muffled heart sounds, distended neck veins and hypotension are tamponade.",
