    rationale: "Pleuritic chest pain that improves when sitting up and leaning forward, accompanied by a pericardial friction rub (scratchy sound produced by inflamed visceral and parietal pericardial layers rubbing together), is the hallmark presentation of acute pericarditis. The 12-lead ECG classically shows diffuse, concave ST-segment elevations across nearly all leads with PR-segment depressions. Why the other options fail: A transmural infarct gives crushing pain that does not improve on leaning forward and is usually accompanied by ischaemic ECG changes; costochondritis is reproducible on palpation and lacks the friction rub; a dissection gives tearing pain radiating to the back. Positional pleuritic pain with a rub is pericarditis.",
    strategy: "Pericarditis hallmarks: sharp chest pain relieved by sitting forward + pericardial friction rub + diffuse ST-elevation across all leads. Treat with NSAIDs and colchicine.",
