    rationale: "Beta-adrenergic blockers (metoprolol) decrease heart rate, myocardial contractility, and blood pressure by blocking beta-1 receptors. Standard clinical hold parameters dictate withholding the medication if the apical heart rate is less than 60 beats/min or systolic blood pressure is less than 90-100 mmHg. Administering a beta-blocker to a bradycardic client (HR 50 bpm) risks severe symptomatic bradycardia, complete heart block, or cardiogenic shock. Why the other options fail: A blood pressure above one hundred does not make the dose safe when the apical rate is below sixty; splitting a sustained-release tablet alters its release and still gives a beta-blocker to a bradycardic client; exercise to raise the rate before giving a rate-slowing drug is not a practice. Withholding, documenting and calling the prescriber is correct.",
